---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../../lib/url.js";
import Places from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Places.astro";
import places from "../../data/places.json";
import people from "../../data/people.json";
const total = people.length;
const rows = places.map((pl) => ({
  name: pl.name, href: "/places/" + pl.slug + "/", n: pl.count,
}));
---
<Base title="Places" eyebrow={`Every place this surname has been found — ${places.length} of them`}
      dek="The same register, arranged by geography rather than by name. It is the quickest way to see where this family's evidence actually clusters — and where it does not.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What the geography says</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Read down this list and the shape of the problem appears. The surname clusters in the
      <strong>River Plate</strong> — Buenos Aires, Montevideo, and a small inland town in Uruguay called
      <strong>Trinidad</strong>, where six men of the name fathered children in the 1870s. That is very
      probably the founding kindred.</p>
    <p>And <strong>Rosario</strong>, the one place the family itself names, is thin. Everything found there
      is either twentieth century or a lodger in a tenement. <strong>The place we were told to look is the
      place with least in it.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <Places rows={rows} u={u} />
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:22px">{total} people in all, across {places.length} places.
    <a href={u("/people/")}>Everyone &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
</Base>
